        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The council sent a man to disconnect my father’s shadow.\\n\\n“He’s been dead eight months,” I said.\\n\\nThe man checked his tablet.\\n\\n“And you’ve continued using it.”\\n\\nBehind me, Dad’s shadow sat at the kitchen table. Mine stood where it should, attached to my feet.\\n\\n“I didn’t know who to call.”\\n\\n“Most people don’t.”\\n\\nHe unpacked a yellow scraper and a roll of black bags.\\n\\nSince the funeral, the shadow had kept Dad’s hours. Downstairs at six. By the back door after lunch. In his armchair until the shipping forecast ended.\\n\\nSometimes I put the radio on for it.\\n\\n“Does it hurt?” I asked.\\n\\n“Not the shadow.”\\n\\nHe moved the table aside. Dad’s outline stayed seated, its hands folded over an invisible stomach.\\n\\nThe man knelt and worked the scraper under one shoe.\\n\\nUpstairs, a floorboard creaked.\\n\\nHe stopped.\\n\\n“Anyone else home?”\\n\\n“No.”\\n\\nAnother creak. Directly above us.\\n\\nThe man lowered the scraper carefully.\\n\\n“Did you let it upstairs?”\\n\\n“It’s his house.”\\n\\nHe looked towards the ceiling.\\n\\n“How many nights?”\\n\\n“Every night.”\\n\\nThe shadow unfolded its hands.\\n\\nThe man packed his tools, leaving the black bags on the floor.\\n\\n“Wait,” I said. “You haven’t finished.”\\n\\n“I can’t disconnect an occupied service.”\\n\\nFrom upstairs came a cough I recognised. Then the squeal of Dad’s bed springs.\\n\\nThe man hurried out, pulling the front door shut behind him.\\n\\nI stood in the kitchen until footsteps reached the landing.\\n\\n“Who was that?” Dad called.\\n\\nHis shadow rose and stretched towards the stairs.\\n\\n“Council,” I said.\\n\\n“Don’t let them in again.”\\n\\nI heard him trying each bedroom door.\\n\\n“Which one’s yours now?”\"\n}",
